 not a legal way  there's no way to actually enforce it  but you'll find that if people you know  get that emotional connection then you  know they may think twice about it so  when you are asked  people for the sale or after they  purchased it you have a thank you page  you can put down as part of your message  if you're really worried about it  happening the next thing you can do is  if you know who is actually doing the  sharing then you can try to reach out to  them and again with a personal message I  wouldn't try to do any kind of legal  action but try to just explain again  you're a small business this is really  hurting you and if they wouldn't mind  taking it down that's only if you know  them often can be hard to find out who  the person is who's sharing it but that  that's a tactic again you're just  appealing to their the good in them and  and it can help if if you can actually  identify who the person is  next thing is potentially doing some  kind of legal action and going after the  websites who are publishing it if it's a  big website like Pirate Bay you know  good luck there's huge companies who are  trying to take them down already if  something just put it up on their own  website you may be able to reach out to  them and say you know can you please  take it down but if it's one of the  bigger file sharing sites you don't  really have a lot of options here there  are some people who will never pay for  products and it really just depends on  how convenient it is for them so even  going to these file sharing sites there  is some work involved versus just  somebody landing on your website and  making that purchase so some people  value their their time a lot more than  their money and some people value their  money a lot more than their time so the  guys who will spend two hours trying to  find a free copy of a five dollar book  they're probably not going to buy from  you right they're not your ideal  customer maybe they might be at some  point it's 